<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lothair III, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1075">1075</a>–<a href="/years/1137">1137</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Lothair of Supplinburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> III, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a> from 1106, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1125 and emperor from 1133. As duke he granted Holstein and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stormarn">Stormarn</a> to Adolf I of Schauenburg in 1111 and encouraged expansion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. His alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welfs</a> shaped German politics after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothair-iii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Lothair III, Holy Roman Emperor" width="267" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothair_II,_Holy_Roman_Emperor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lothair was the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> count Gebhard of Supplinburg, who joined the Saxon rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-dynasty">Salian dynasty</a> and died on 9 June 1075 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-langensalza-1075">Battle of Langensalza</a>. Lothair was born shortly afterwards at Unterlüß, and little is known of his youth. In 1107 he married Richenza, daughter of Count Henry of Northeim and heiress of the Brunonids. Land purchases, inheritance and marriage alliances brought him the domains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-billung">House of Billung</a> and the Counts of Northeim, making him the wealthiest nobleman among the Saxons. He supported Henry V's rebellion against Henry IV, and was rewarded with the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> when Duke Magnus of Billung died without an heir in 1106.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As duke, Lothair joined the opposition to Henry V and in 1115 commanded the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> forces that defeated the emperor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-welfesholz">Battle of Welfesholz</a>. After Henry's death in 1125 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and passed over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> candidate, Duke Frederick, who refused to accept free election. Lothair was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at over fifty years of age and without a male heir, and his election marked a departure from hereditary succession, as the electors preferred a ruler of moderate powers after the Salian era. A war with the Hohenstaufens followed, and the Swabians and Franconians declared Frederick's brother Conrad anti-king. In 1129 Lothair took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and Speyer, and in 1135 he defeated the Hohenstaufens, who were pardoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>.</p><p>In 1130 a papal schism arose between Innocent II and Anacletus II. Bernard of Clairvaux persuaded Lothair to favour Innocent, who crowned him in the Lateran Basilica on 4 June 1133, since Anacletus held St Peter's. In the north and east he made Albert the Bear Margrave of Brandenburg in 1134 and Conrad of Wettin Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> in 1136. His campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> in 1136 and 1137 captured Capua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lothair died in December 1137 on the return journey at Breitenwang, having given his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-proud">Henry the Proud</a> the Margraviate of Tuscany, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and the imperial insignia. His bones were taken to the Collegiate Church of Saints Peter and Paul at Königslutter, for which he had laid the cornerstone in 1135. His only surviving child, Gertrude, was married to Henry the Proud in 1127, and their son was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>. After Lothair's death the Hohenstaufen Conrad was elected king as Conrad III and deprived Henry the Proud of all his territories, ending Lothair's plan for a Welf kingship.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lothair III, Holy Roman Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Lothair of Supplinburg</b> then became duke and, in 1125, king; his heir was his son-in-law Henry the Proud of the Welf family.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The new kingdom had to be defended by force: Pope Innocent II, Emperor <b>Lothair III</b> and rebellious mainland barons and cities fought Roger for most of the 1130s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1134 Emperor <b>Lothair III</b> gave the Northern March to Albert the Bear of the House of Ascania; Albert had an understanding with Pribislav-Henry, the Christian prince of the Hevelli, who made him his heir, and on Pribislav’s death in 1150 he took over the fortress of Brandenburg…</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1111 <b>Duke Lothair of Saxony</b> granted the County of Holstein and Stormarn to Adolf I of Schauenburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holstein">County of Holstein</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1111 <b>Duke Lothair of Saxony</b> granted Adolf I of Schauenburg the County of Holstein, beginning a long double rule over Holstein and the Weser lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-schaumburg">County of Schaumburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Unterlüß, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Breitenwang, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.85,10.29&amp;year=1115" aria-label="Unterlüß on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4780 343 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lothair III, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5230 613h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5230 613h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.85,10.29&amp;year=1115">What was here in 1115? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
